Your task is to complete the implementation of the `CircularBuffer` class by adding the following methods:
1. `write`: This method should write `n` bytes from the given buffer `buf` into the circular buffer. If the circular buffer is full, the oldest data should be overwritten.
2. `is_full`: This method should return `true` if the circular buffer is full, and `false` otherwise.
3. `is_empty`: This method should return `true` if the circular buffer is empty, and `false` otherwise.

You should also define the constant `BUFFER_SIZE` representing the size of the circular buffer.

Your implementation should ensure that the circular buffer behaves as described above, overwriting the oldest data when it is full and correctly indicating whether it is full or empty.

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>

class CircularBuffer : public Reader, public Writer {
  static const uint32_t BUFFER_SIZE = 1024;
  uint32_t last_sender_id_;
  uint32_t n_callbacks_logged_;
  uint32_t dummy_wr_;
  uint32_t dummy_rd_;
  uint8_t dummy_buff_[BUFFER_SIZE];

public:
  CircularBuffer() : last_sender_id_(), n_callbacks_logged_(), dummy_wr_(), dummy_rd_(), dummy_buff_() {
    set_reader(this);
    set_writer(this);
  }

  s32 read(uint8_t *buf, const uint32_t n) override {
    uint32_t real_n = n;
    memcpy(buf, dummy_buff_ + dummy_rd_, real_n);
    // Your implementation here
  }

  void write(const uint8_t *buf, const uint32_t n) {
    if (n <= BUFFER_SIZE) {
      if (dummy_wr_ + n <= BUFFER_SIZE) {
        memcpy(dummy_buff_ + dummy_wr_, buf, n);
        dummy_wr_ = (dummy_wr_ + n) % BUFFER_SIZE;
      } else {
        uint32_t first_chunk = BUFFER_SIZE - dummy_wr_;
        memcpy(dummy_buff_ + dummy_wr_, buf, first_chunk);
        memcpy(dummy_buff_, buf + first_chunk, n - first_chunk);
        dummy_wr_ = n - first_chunk;
      }
    } else {
      // Handle error: buffer size exceeded
    }
  }

  bool is_full() {
    return (dummy_wr_ + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE == dummy_rd_;
  }

  bool is_empty() {
    return dummy_wr_ == dummy_rd_;
  }
};
```

In the solution, the `write` method writes the given buffer into the circular buffer, handling the case where the buffer is full and overwriting the oldest data. The `is_full` and `is_empty` methods correctly determine whether the circular buffer is full or empty based on the positions of the read and write pointers.

