```cpp
vector<int> MaxK(int arr[], int n, int k) {
    vector<int> result;
    deque<int> Q(k);
    int i = 0;

    // Process the first subarray of size k
    for (i; i < k; i++) {
        while (!Q.empty() && arr[i] >= arr[Q.back()]) {
            Q.pop_back();
        }
        Q.push_back(i);
    }

    // Process the remaining subarrays
    for (i; i < n; i++) {
        result.push_back(arr[Q.front()]);  // Store the maximum element of the previous subarray

        // Remove elements outside the current subarray from the front of the deque
        while (!Q.empty() && Q.front() <= i - k) {
            Q.pop_front();
        }

        // Remove elements smaller than the current element from the back of the deque
        while (!Q.empty() && arr[i] >= arr[Q.back()]) {
            Q.pop_back();
        }

        Q.push_back(i);  // Add the current element to the deque
    }

    result.push_back(arr[Q.front()]);  // Store the maximum element of the last subarray

    return result;
}
```

The `MaxK` function uses a deque to efficiently find the maximum element in each subarray of size `k`. It iterates through the array, maintaining a deque that stores the indices of elements in the current subarray. At each step, it updates the deque to ensure that it only contains relevant indices for the current subarray and efficiently finds the maximum element. Finally, it returns a vector containing the maximum elements of each subarray.

