Your task is to complete the `triangleIsIntersected` function, which takes a `Ray` object representing a ray in 3D space, and two output parameters: `distAlongRayToHit` (the distance along the ray to the intersection point) and `hitPoint` (the intersection point). The function should return `true` if the ray intersects the triangle, and `false` otherwise.

The `Triangle` class has a member variable `faceNormal` representing the normal vector of the triangle's plane. You can assume that the `Ray` and `Point` classes are already defined and have appropriate member functions and operators for vector operations.

Your implementation should accurately determine if the given ray intersects the triangle and, if so, calculate the intersection point and the distance along the ray to the intersection.

```cpp
bool Triangle::triangleIsIntersected(const Ray& ray, float& distAlongRayToHit, Point& hitPoint) const
{
    const float EPSILON = 0.000001f;
    Vector edge1 = vertex2 - vertex1;
    Vector edge2 = vertex3 - vertex1;
    Vector h = cross(ray.direction, edge2);
    float a = dot(edge1, h);

    if (a > -EPSILON && a < EPSILON) {
        return false;  // The ray is parallel to the triangle
    }

    float f = 1.0f / a;
    Vector s = ray.origin - vertex1;
    float u = f * dot(s, h);

    if (u < 0.0f || u > 1.0f) {
        return false;
    }

    Vector q = cross(s, edge1);
    float v = f * dot(ray.direction, q);

    if (v < 0.0f || u + v > 1.0f) {
        return false;
    }

    float t = f * dot(edge2, q);

    if (t > EPSILON) {
        distAlongRayToHit = t;
        hitPoint = ray.origin + ray.direction * t;
        return true;
    }

    return false;
}
```

In the solution, we use the Möller–Trumbore intersection algorithm to determine if the ray intersects the triangle. We calculate the barycentric coordinates (u, v) of the intersection point and use them to determine if the intersection lies within the triangle. If an intersection is found, we calculate the distance along the ray to the intersection point and the intersection point itself. If no intersection is found, we return `false`.

